// PENDING: from an OPEN SigmaHQ pull request. Not reviewed or merged by SigmaHQ yet.
// Pull request: #5800 add: Linux Security Capability Set Via Setfattr Utility
// Pull request URL: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/pull/5800
// Pull request author: EzLucky
// Change: new rule
// Title: Linux Security Capability Set via Setfattr Utility
// Sigma ID: 768cc7c9-b039-44f2-8ac3-3c370e58b03f
// Status: experimental | Level: medium
// Author: Luc Génaux
// Date: 2026-10-02 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: privilege-escalation, persistence, T1548, T1554
// Description: Detects the use of the 'setfattr' utility to modify security capabilities of a file. The
//   capability feature in Linux allows executable files to obtain specific permissions without
//   needing traditional root privileges, which can be exploited for unauthorized access, privileges
//   escalation, or to bypass security controls. Unlike the standard 'setcap' utility, the use of
//   'setfattr' to modify security capabilities is not common in normal operations and could indicate
//   stealthy and suspicious activity.
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/setfattr.1.html
//   - https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/capabilities.7.html
//   - https://yunolay.com/linux-capabilities-abuse
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/6eb55cbcd6eeeb64c807d5d41f8548cae4d00fbd/rules/linux/process_creation/proc_creation_lnx_setfattr_sec_cap.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "/setfattr" and ProcessCommandLine contains "security.capability"
